{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiiZFb39qTyDhPEqx6AS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and the self-check passed (`OK`). It fits much worse than the current best model, and it may be too slow for the 30-minute admission limit.\n\n**`exemplar_random_vs_designed_ratio`** (in `iter_2/candidate_0/`)\n\n- **Hypothesis:** people remember examples of two kinds of sequence and sort a new one by which kind it resembles more:\n  - **Designed:** streaks, perfect alternation, and 3- or 4-flip motifs repeated.\n  - **Random-looking:** heads and tails balanced within one flip, no long streak, not perfect alternation.\n  \n  Similarity falls off exponentially with the number of flips that don't match, so the closest examples count most. A sequence looks random to the extent its total similarity to the random examples outweighs its total similarity to the designed ones. The person picks the sequence that wins on that ratio.\n- **How it differs from the pruned `designed_exemplar_similarity`:** that model only remembered designed examples. This one adds a remembered random category and judges by the ratio between the two, as in standard exemplar models of categorisation.\n- **Parameters:** one similarity steepness, plus decisiveness that varies by person (drawn from a population, with 400 slots so the next design can predict new participants). Features are counts of examples at each distance from 0 to 8, worked out from the raw sequences once per pair.\n\n**Self-check results**\n- **Fit:** the smoke-fit ELPD-LOO was −3004.7, against −2105.7 for the best model. That's a short single-chain fit, not a real comparison, but the gap is large.\n- **Loo reliability:** PSIS-LOO came out unreliable on the smoke fit (1.6% of trials had high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance).\n- **Convergence:** not verified, because one chain can't give an R-hat.\n- **Speed:** the 200-iteration smoke fit took 216 